<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad bin Tughluq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1290–1351</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Ulugh Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad bin Tughluq was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> from 1325 to 1351. As the prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-khan">Ulugh Khan</a> he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> in 1323, ending the Kakatiya dynasty. His reign brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> to its greatest extent, but schemes such as moving the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Daulatabad</a> and a token <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> currency failed, and revolts founded independent states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq.jpg" alt="Muhammad bin Tughluq" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_bin_Tughlaq_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
